TI  - Geopolymer based on spent fluid catalytic cracking catalyst: Design optimisation and high-temperature resistance
PY  - 2025
T2  - Case Studies in Construction Materials
VL  - 22
C7  - e04395
DO  - 10.1016/j.cscm.2025.e04395
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85217792140&doi=10.1016%2fj.cscm.2025.e04395&partnerID=40&md5=8994b76a13358a1cc8dc119a31b32842
AB  - Geopolymers made with wastes: spent fluid catalytic cracking catalyst (FCC), ground-granulated-blast-furnace slag (GGBS) and fly ash (FA) are studied. The optimal activator composition and FCC: GGBS/FA ratio are determined, and geopolymers with an excellent performance created. The FCC-GGBS system exhibits exceptional robustness, performing well across a range of activators ([Na+] = 6–10 mol/L; SiO2/Na2O = 1.4–1.0) and producing outstanding strength (45–57 MPa at 28d). Low [Na+] and SiO2/Na2O ratios can be safely used to fabricate FCC-GGBS geopolymer to lower cost and embodied carbon. The geopolymers were studied with X-ray diffraction (XRD), Fourier-transform infrared spectrometry (FTIR) and Scanning electron microscopy (SEM/EDX). XRD and FTIR concur, showing that the zeolite comprising FCC dissolves and participates in polymerization reactions which form cement with Si-O-Si(Al) links of varying degrees of polymerization. SEM/EDX show dense matrices with no unreacted particles and amorphous cement of high Si/Al/Na/Ca concurring with C(N)-A-S-H which agrees with the superior strength of FCC-GGBS geopolymer. Conversely, FA is unsuitable for FCC-geopolymer fabrication due to a high sensitivity to activator composition. Deviation from optimal composition impairs strength, and FA content over a threshold (40–60 %) fails to develop strength. FCC-FA geopolymer shows unreacted particles and coarse-grained matrices indicating poor activation and agreeing with its inferior strength. High-temperature resistance of FCC-GGBS geopolymer is investigated. Calcination caused devitrification, destruction of C-A-S-H and N-A-S-H cements and their transformation into aluminosilicates nepheline/gehlenite. Volume change on phase transformation, coupled with differential thermal expansion gel-aggregate caused cracking and significant strength reduction. © 2025 The Authors
