AB  - This study presents a geopolymer-based Subaqueous Foamed Lightweight Soil (SFLS), innovatively facilitating the application of Foamed Lightweight Soil (FLS) for subaqueous backfilling and enabling waste spoil (WS) recycling on a large scale. Key findings include that modified SFLS with dispersants is suitable for subaqueous use, achieving over 90 % of its 28-day unconfined compressive strength subaqueous compared to in air. SFLS offers advantages such as low wet density, excellent flowability, and adjustable strength (1–2 MPa). The synergistic interaction of geopolymers, Sodium Lauryl Ether Sulfate (SLES), and Polyacrylamide (PAM) enhances its performance, with the geopolymer reaction's alkaline environment improving fluidity and foam resilience. PAM's addition boosts dispersion resistance and foam stabilization, ensuring uniformity and lightweight benefits, thus fortifying SFLS's durability. With a density close to water, SFLS minimizes additional underwater stress, showcasing superior sustainability by significantly reducing costs and CO2 emissions, indicating its potential for widespread application in sustainable construction practices. © 2025 The Authors
