TI  - Enhancement of strength and microstructural characterization in red mud-iron carbonate binders: Effects of CO2 sequestration and oxalic acid concentration
PY  - 2025
T2  - Results in Engineering
VL  - 25
C7  - 104539
DO  - 10.1016/j.rineng.2025.104539
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85219661440&doi=10.1016%2fj.rineng.2025.104539&partnerID=40&md5=c9cec313e8bd609aa767113c296ecf73
AB  - This study explores the use of industrial waste products and carbon sequestration techniques to examine sustainable alternatives to traditional cement-based building materials. Previous studies demonstrate the performance of iron carbonate-based binders in the presence of fly ash and other pozzolanic material, but red mud (RM) as an addition to the iron carbonate matrix is a novel approach, which this study has aimed to address. This investigation primarily uses iron powder, red mud, metakaolin, and calcium carbonate. Oxalic acid is added at varying concentrations (0.5 M, 1 M, 1.5 M, and 2 M) to the binder matrix, and CO2 curing is performed for 3 days and 6 days. The CO2-cured samples are compared with non-cured samples. The samples are tested for their flow, strength, and microstructural characterization. Results indicate that higher oxalic acid concentrations (1.5 M and 2 M) increase viscosity and reduce flowability, while 0.5 M oxalic acid enhances the binder's workability. The compressive strength is the highest in 0.5 M oxalic acid after 3 days of CO2 curing and 1 M oxalic acid after 6 days of CO2 curing. This study shows that lower oxalic acid concentrations and extended CO2 curing foster the formation of dense carbonate phases, such as siderite and calcite, enhancing matrix density and reducing pores, thereby enhancing strength development. © 2025 The Authors
