TI  - Towards decarbonization in the building industry: Understanding the hydration mechanism of cementitious binders with the incorporation of coal bottom ash
PY  - 2025
T2  - Journal of Building Engineering
VL  - 103
C7  - 112097
DO  - 10.1016/j.jobe.2025.112097
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85217742279&doi=10.1016%2fj.jobe.2025.112097&partnerID=40&md5=8d60355d20b3b4b5d097d27ae1088195
AB  - Coal bottom ash (CBA) is often disposed of in ash ponds or landfills, creating environmental challenges. While its use in concrete has been explored, investigation of its application in cement production, a key aspect of decarbonizing the building industry, remains limited. This study discusses the hydration mechanism of cementitious binders incorporating CBA to facilitate its feasible application in cementitious composites. Sustainable formulations of cementitious binders were synthesized through detailed chemical, microstructural, and mechanical analyses. These analyses demonstrated a comprehensive correlation, providing insights into the hydration mechanism within CBA-incorporating cementitious binders. Two types of cement-based composites were selected to be compared against the control (OPC100-CBA0); i.e. OPC80-CBA20 (20 % CBA as OPC substitution) and OPC60-CBA40 (40 % CBA as OPC substitution). OPC80-CBA20 exhibited compressive strength improvements of up to 26 % compared to the control, while OPC60-CBA40 showed reductions of up to 24 %. These findings were validated through chemical and microstructural analyses. OPC80-CBA20 revealed extensive portlandite replacement by calcium-silicate-hydrate and calcium-aluminate-silicate-hydrate gels, followed by OPC60-CBA40, while OPC100-CBA0 retained the highest amount of portlandite. The experimental results aligned with codal requirements, facilitating the use of CBA-incorporating cementitious binders within the cement industry. Sustainability and cost analyses indicated that the incorporation of CBA in cementitious binder significantly reduced CO2 emissions and material costs, supporting decarbonization efforts in the building industry as an eco-friendly and cost-efficient option. © 2025 The Authors
