TI  - Performance analysis of geopolymer-carbon fiber stabilized steel slag base layer
PY  - 2025
T2  - Polymer Testing
VL  - 146
C7  - 108766
DO  - 10.1016/j.polymertesting.2025.108766
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105000502221&doi=10.1016%2fj.polymertesting.2025.108766&partnerID=40&md5=b2b1c640e511ac4ab765ff751541ed21
AB  - In order to promote the large-scale synergistic utilization of multi-source massive solid waste in road base, fly ash and solid waste residue were used to prepare ground polymer instead of cement as cementitious material of cement stabilized base. The effects of carbon fiber content, steel slag content and different cementitious materials on the properties of base materials were discussed. The results show that: after adding carbon fiber, the unconfined compressive strength, splitting strength and bending and tensile strength of the base material show an increasing trend, respectively, improved by 12 %–20 %; the mechanical properties of the steel slag doped base material show a trend of increasing and then decreasing, and the mechanical properties reach the peak at 60 % dosage; compared with cement stabilized material, the geopolymer stabilized material has a slight decrease in mechanical properties, but the overall The mechanical properties of the ground polymer stabilized material decreased slightly compared with the cement stabilized material, but the overall properties remained stable. The incorporation of carbon fiber positively affects the drying and frost resistance of the base material; the drying and frost resistance of the base material reaches the best state when the dosage of steel slag is 60 %, but the frost resistance is obviously reduced when the dosage of steel slag is increased to 100 %; when geopolymer substitutes cement as the cementitious material, the drying and frost resistance of the base material are significantly improved. The addition of carbon fibers forms a fiber network structure, which enhances the mechanical properties and durability of the ground polymer-stabilized steel slag base materials; the active minerals on the surface of steel slag aggregates react with alkali and silicate, which promotes the geopolymerization reaction and further facilitates the generation and development of the hydration gel, and establishes a stable internal structure to support the strength of the base materials; the irregular surface morphology of ground polymer results in the detachment of some of the hydration products from the surface of the sample, which reduces the bonding properties of the base materials. The irregular surface morphology of the geopolymer leads to the detachment of some hydration products from the specimen surface, which reduces its adhesion, and the disintegration, precipitation and gradual hardening of silicon and aluminum monomers stimulated in the alkaline environment, which results in the volumetric shrinkage and the formation of microcracks, and reduces the strength of the base material. © 2025
